OCGRAPH 1
DOMAIN gameplay
# THE GAME MODE, as a graph. A round clock in a VAR is already a complete demonstration of "a game mode
# is a graph class, so the play session starts", and there is nothing first-person-specific to add.
#
# YOU WILL SEE TWO OF THESE TICKING in a headless log, not one, even though Default.ocmap places
# exactly one. That is not this graph misbehaving: aver_fw_begin_play spawns a FRESH instance of
# whatever class carries the GameMode flag the moment play begins, unconditionally, with no awareness
# that the level's own PLACE line already put one down. Both tick off the same per-frame deltaTime, so
# their `elapsed` values track each other exactly. Harmless -- neither instance's state reaches the
# other's -- but worth naming rather than leaving you to wonder if you miscounted your placements.
#
# IT NAMES ITS OWN PAWN AND CONTROLLER, which is what makes this a playable first-person game rather
# than a level with a character standing in it. `pawn=` and `controller=` are resolved by name at seal
# and handed to aver_fw_begin_play, which spawns both and POSSESSES the pawn. Possession is the whole
# point: GameApp's camera follows aver_fw_controlled_pawn() and gives up when there is none, so before
# these existed the player was placed in the map, never possessed, and the shipped game rendered from
# a default camera however loudly the character asked for `view=firstperson`.
#
# BOTH ARE REQUIRED. begin_play possesses only when it has BOTH, and the built-in PlayerController is
# ABSTRACT and so unspawnable -- naming a pawn alone spawns it and leaves it unpossessed, which looks
# wired up and does nothing. AN_FPController.ocgraph exists solely to be the concrete controller.
#
# THE PLAYER IS NO LONGER PLACED IN THE MAP. The mode spawns it, exactly the way a UE GameMode spawns
# its DefaultPawnClass -- leaving the PLACE line in as well would put two characters in the level, one
# possessed and one not.
#
# AND WHY THERE IS NO COMBINED SCORE HERE. Each target owns its own `score` VAR and that is genuinely
# the score. A single total would need one of two things this engine does not have yet: FireEvent
# carries NO PAYLOAD (the receiving ENTRY gets entity/time/deltaTime and nothing else, so a target
# cannot report an amount back), and this mode has no way to enumerate the targets to poll them --
# they are placed by the map, and nothing hands a graph a list of entities by class. Stating that
# plainly is better than a total that quietly counts wrong.
NAME FPRules
DESCRIPTION The game mode, as a graph. Starts the play session and keeps a round clock in a VAR.

CLASS AN_FPRules GameMode pawn=AN_FPCharacter controller=AN_FPController

PARAM deltaTime float

VAR elapsed float 0

NODE dt Param param=deltaTime

ENTRY tick OnTick
NODE tick OnTick

NODE prev GetVar var=elapsed
NODE sum Add
LINK prev.value sum.a
LINK dt.value sum.b

NODE store SetVar var=elapsed
LINK tick.exec store.exec
LINK sum.result store.value

# Read back through its own node after the write, for the reason AN_FPTarget.ocgraph gives.
NODE report GetVar var=elapsed
OUT report value
